# SYNOPSIS

```usage
tr [-cCds] set1 [set2]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`tr` copies standard input to standard output, changing characters as it goes.
Characters are UTF-8 runes, not bytes.

With two sets and no flags, each character in <set1> is replaced by the character at the same position in <set2>.
If <set2> is shorter, its last character stands for the rest.

: `-c`, `-C`
  Use the complement of <set1>: every character not in it.
  When translating, each such character becomes the last character of <set2>.
: `-d`
  Delete the characters in <set1>.
  Only <set1> is given.
: `-s`
  Squeeze: replace a run of one repeated character by a single one.
  With <set1> alone, the runs squeezed are of characters in <set1>; with two sets, `tr` translates and squeezes runs of characters in <set2>.

Without `-d` or `-s` both sets are required.
`-d` and `-s` together are refused.

A set is a string of these, which may be mixed:

: <c>
  The character itself.
: `\`<c>
  An escape: `\\`, `\'`, `\"`, `\a`, `\b`, `\e` or `\E` (escape), `\f`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\v`, `\` and one to three octal digits, or `\x` and one or two hexadecimal digits.
  Any other is an error.
: <c>`-`<d>
  The characters from <c> to <d>, which must not come before <c>.
: `[:`<class>`:]`
  A character class: `alnum`, `alpha`, `blank`, `cntrl`, `digit`, `graph`, `lower`, `print`, `punct`, `space`, `upper` or `xdigit`.
  A character of <set1> matched by a class is replaced by the last character of <set2>.
  In <set2> only `[:lower:]` and `[:upper:]` are allowed, and they map case: `tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]'` capitalises.
: `[=`<c>`=]`
  The character <c> alone.
: `[`<c>`*`<n>`]`
  <c> repeated <n> times; <n> is octal if it starts with `0`, and an <n> of `0` or none means the length of <set1>.

`tr` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/tr`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/tr`.
There is no native `tr`.

# EXAMPLES

sbase's test (`tests/posix/sbasetest.c`) runs `tr a-z A-Z` with `hello` on standard input, and gets `HELLO`.

# SEE ALSO

sed(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/tr.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

A malformed set is reported as `tr: Invalid character class.` or `tr: invalid escape sequence` and the escape; a <set2> that is empty, or holds a class other than `lower` and `upper`, as `tr: cannot map to an empty set.` or `tr: can only map to 'lower' and 'upper' class.`.
These, a read or write error, and the usage message exit 1.

# BUGS

A NUL cannot be in a set: `\0` ends the set there.

`-d` with `-s`, which POSIX allows, is refused.
`-c` does not reach `-s` with <set1> alone: `tr -cs` <set1> squeezes the characters in <set1>, not those outside it.

`[=`<c>`=]` is no more than <c>: there are no equivalence classes.
`[`<c>`*`<n>`]` is taken in <set1> too, where POSIX does not allow it.

Only `tr a-z A-Z` has been run on VectraOS; the rest is untested here.
